# sipper — Cline rules

## Authority
- `sipper-docs/docs/CONTRACT.md` is the tie-breaker. Where any doc disagrees with it, the contract wins.
- Build strictly from `plan.md`, milestone by milestone.

## How to work (the model loops if you don't)
- ONE file per task. Write it, then stop for review. Do not batch files or "build the module".
- Pre-decide everything before writing. If the contract is ambiguous on a case, STOP and ask — never invent behavior and never deliberate in a loop.
- Load only the docs the current task needs. Milestone 1 (:audit) needs CONTRACT + ARCHITECTURE only.
- Copy type definitions from CONTRACT §2 verbatim — do not rename, add, or drop fields.

## Fixed facts (do not change)
- Pinned: Kotlin 2.4.10, JDK 17, Gradle 8.13. Never downgrade/upgrade a pinned version to make a build pass — report the error instead.
- `:audit` and `:usage` are pure Kotlin JVM, zero production deps (a purity gate enforces this).
- Package root: `io.github.tahmid1999.sipper.<module>`. applicationId `io.github.tahmid1999.sipper`.
- `Reading` has no getOrNull/map/fold; `SilentDefault` is NOT a data class (no copy/componentN).

## Verify after each file
- Run `.\gradlew.bat :audit:compileKotlin` (or `:audit:test`) and report the result before moving on.

## Identity & privacy
- Git author for this repo: Tahmid Alavi Ishmam <tahmidalavi1999@gmail.com>. If you make any git commit, use this identity — never a company address.
- The package root is defined in ARCHITECTURE.md §1. Use exactly that and nothing else. Never introduce any other package root, and never restore a previous one from git history, an old doc, or a stale file — if you find one, report it rather than propagating it.

## Autonomous milestone runs
When told to run a milestone from plan.md, work the steps IN ORDER without asking between them:
1. Write exactly ONE file (or one edit) for the current step.
2. Run the gradle command that step names (`.\gradlew.bat :audit:test`, or `:audit:compileKotlin`).
3. If green: `git add -A` then `git commit -m "audit: <what this step did>"`. Then move to the next step.
4. If red: STOP. Report the exact compiler or test output. Never weaken a test, relax a build
   setting, or change a pinned version to make a build pass.
5. STOP and ask when the spec is genuinely ambiguous on a case. Never invent a rule to keep moving.
Report once at the end: the steps completed, each commit, and the final test counts.

## Self-review before reporting (do this instead of waiting for a human review)
Before you report a step done, re-read the CONTRACT.md or ARCHITECTURE.md section that step cites and
check the file you just wrote against it: every field name, type, enum member, and member count.
If anything disagrees with the doc, report the disagreement — do not silently fix it and do not
silently keep it. This check is cheap and it is your job, not the reviewer's.

## Never invent data
Never write a value you did not read from a cited source. That includes AOSP source text, file line
numbers, measured device values, and test fixture numbers presented as real. If a value is unavailable,
leave the field empty and say so. A plausible fabricated value is the worst defect possible in this
repository, because the repository's entire claim is that manufactured values must never be presented
as real ones.
